DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       These  system calls change the owner and group of a file.  The cchhoowwnn(),
       ffcchhoowwnn(), and llcchhoowwnn() system calls differ only  in  how  the  file  is
       specified:

       •  cchhoowwnn()  changes  the ownership of the file specified by _p_a_t_h, which
          is dereferenced if it is a symbolic link.

       •  ffcchhoowwnn() changes the ownership of the file referred to by  the  open
          file descriptor _f_d.

       •  llcchhoowwnn() is like cchhoowwnn(), but does not dereference symbolic links.

       Only  a  privileged  process (Linux: one with the CCAAPP__CCHHOOWWNN capability)
       may change the owner of a file.  The owner of a  file  may  change  the
       group  of  the  file  to  any group of which that owner is a member.  A
       privileged process (Linux: with CCAAPP__CCHHOOWWNN) may change the  group  arbi‐
       trarily.

       If the _o_w_n_e_r or _g_r_o_u_p is specified as -1, then that ID is not changed.

       When the owner or group of an executable file is changed by an unprivi‐
       leged  user, the SS__IISSUUIIDD and SS__IISSGGIIDD mode bits are cleared.  POSIX does
       not specify whether this also should happen when root does the cchhoowwnn();
       the Linux behavior depends on  the  kernel  version,  and  since  Linux
       2.2.13,  root is treated like other users.  In case of a non‐group‐exe‐
       cutable file (i.e., one for which the SS__IIXXGGRRPP bit is not set) the SS__IISS‐‐
       GGIIDD bit indicates mandatory locking, and is not cleared by a cchhoowwnn().

       When the owner or group of an executable file is changed (by any user),
       all capability sets for the file are cleared.

   ffcchhoowwnnaatt(())
       The ffcchhoowwnnaatt() system call operates in exactly the same way as cchhoowwnn(),
       except for the differences described here.

       If _p_a_t_h is relative, then it is interpreted relative to  the  directory
       referred  to  by the file descriptor _d_i_r_f_d (rather than relative to the
       current working directory of the calling process, as is done by cchhoowwnn()
       for a relative pathname).

       If _p_a_t_h is relative and _d_i_r_f_d is the special value AATT__FFDDCCWWDD, then  _p_a_t_h
       is interpreted relative to the current working directory of the calling
       process (like cchhoowwnn()).

       If _p_a_t_h is absolute, then _d_i_r_f_d is ignored.

       The _f_l_a_g_s argument is a bit mask created by ORing together 0 or more of
       the following values;

       AATT__EEMMPPTTYY__PPAATTHH (since Linux 2.6.39)
              If  _p_a_t_h  is an empty string, operate on the file referred to by
              _d_i_r_f_d (which may have been obtained  using  the  ooppeenn(2)  OO__PPAATTHH
              flag).   In  this case, _d_i_r_f_d can refer to any type of file, not
              just a directory.  If _d_i_r_f_d is AATT__FFDDCCWWDD, the  call  operates  on
              the current working directory.  This flag is Linux‐specific; de‐
              fine __GGNNUU__SSOOUURRCCEE to obtain its definition.

       AATT__SSYYMMLLIINNKK__NNOOFFOOLLLLOOWW
              If _p_a_t_h is a symbolic link, do not dereference it: instead oper‐
              ate  on the link itself, like llcchhoowwnn().  (By default, ffcchhoowwnnaatt()
              dereferences symbolic links, like cchhoowwnn().)

       See ooppeennaatt(2) for an explanation of the need for ffcchhoowwnnaatt().

